<p>For a closer look at the difference on storm losses, read <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims/">public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims</a>.</p>

<h2>How Texas regulates public adjusters</h2>
<p>Public adjusting is a regulated profession in Texas. Public adjusters are licensed and overseen by the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. A few rules matter most to policyholders:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Written contract.</strong> Representation starts only with a written contract using TDI-approved terms. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</li>
<li><strong>72-hour rescission.</strong> You may cancel the contract within 72 hours of signing it.</li>
<li><strong>"We represent the insured only."</strong> Texas public adjuster contracts must state this plainly. We do not work for insurers.</li>
<li><strong>No repair conflicts.</strong> A public adjuster may not take part in repairing or rebuilding a property on a claim they adjust.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can verify any public adjuster's license through TDI's online license lookup before you sign anything. We encourage it.</p>

<h2>Our approach</h2>
<p>We do not use shortcuts or one-size-fits-all claim packages. Every property, policy, and loss is handled on its own facts. Our process typically includes:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Inspection.</strong> A thorough look at visible and hidden damage, including roofs, interiors, and systems.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> Photos, measurements, and notes, often supported by Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Policy review.</strong> Reading your declarations, coverages, endorsements, deductibles, and exclusions.</li>
<li><strong>Claim presentation.</strong> A detailed estimate and supporting file submitted to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation and follow-up.</strong> Communicating with the insurer, answering requests, and pursuing <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> or <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> when appropriate.</li>
</ol>
<p>We stay involved from start to finish. You will know who is handling your claim and what happens next.</p>

<h2>Transparency you can count on</h2>
<p>One of the biggest frustrations policyholders describe is not knowing where their claim stands. We make communication a priority:</p>
<ul>
<li>Honest assessments of a claim's strengths and limitations</li>
<li>Regular updates as the claim moves forward</li>
<li>Clear explanations of timelines and next steps</li>
<li>Straight answers, even when they are not what you hoped to hear</li>
</ul>
<p>Sometimes the honest answer is that a public adjuster is not needed. If your insurer's offer looks fair or the loss is small compared with your deductible, we will tell you.</p>

<h2>Ethics: what we never do and what we do</h2>
<p>We never:</p>
<ul>
<li>Inflate damages or add items that are not part of the loss</li>
<li>Misrepresent facts to an insurer</li>
<li>Promise a specific settlement amount or outcome</li>
<li>Pressure anyone into signing a contract</li>
</ul>
<p>We do:</p>
<ul>
<li>Present legitimate claims thoroughly and accurately</li>
<li>Respond to unfair or unsupported denials with documentation</li>
<li>Hold the claim to the terms of the policy you paid for</li>
</ul>

<h2>What we do not do</h2>
<p>Knowing our limits protects you. <strong>We are not attorneys.</strong> We do not give legal advice, file lawsuits, or handle bad-faith litigation; if your situation needs a lawyer, we will say so and suggest you consult a Texas attorney. <strong>We are not contractors.</strong> We do not perform repairs, and we do not take a role in rebuilding a property on a claim we adjust. You keep full control over who repairs your home or building.</p>

<h2>When to call a public adjuster</h2>
<p>Some people call too late, after a settlement has been accepted on incomplete information. Others call early and avoid common mistakes. Consider reaching out if:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your claim was <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied</a> or <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid</a></li>
<li>The insurer keeps <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delaying the claim</a> or changing adjusters</li>
<li>You are being asked for extensive documentation and are unsure what to send</li>
<li>The loss is large, complex, or involves hidden damage</li>
<li>You want an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">independent review of the insurer's estimate</a> before accepting it</li>
</ul>
<p>Help is available at most stages of a claim, though policy deadlines still apply.</p>

<h2>Our commitment to McAllen</h2>
<p>McAllen property owners face real weather risks: spring hail that peaks in April and May, severe straight-line winds, tropical systems such as Hurricane Dolly in 2008 and Hurricane Hanna in 2020, flash flooding, and the February 2021 freeze. Many local homes are slab-on-grade with composition shingle roofs and stucco or brick veneer; many businesses have metal or low-slope roofs. Understanding how those buildings get damaged, and how insurers tend to evaluate that damage, is part of the work.</p>
